3 STAE Poitou-Charentes
3 CTRY France
1 OCCU Diplomat and publicist for the French Protestants, or Huguenots, during the French Wars of Religion (1562-98). Governor of Saumur
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * he was the second eldest son (and therefore as ' second ' son  predestined for the church, which 'roman' church he rejected )  and had 3 brothers & 2 sisters
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.museeprotestant.org/en/notice/philippe-de-mornay-also-called-duplessis-mornay-1549-1623/ Short introduction to Philippe de Mpornay by: museeprotestant.org]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.monarchie-noblesse.net/noblesse/france/montmorency/montmorency.htm  Genealogical Tree-The House of Montmorency incl. Philippe de Mornay ]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Philippe de Mornay, Seigneur du Plessis-Marly===
2 CONT
2 CONT (aka Duplessis-Mornay) Philippe de Mornay was born in Buhy, Normandy, on November 5, 1549. '''His mother had Protestant leanings, but his father tried to counteract her influence by sending him to the College de Lisieux in Paris.'
2 CONC '' After the death of his father in 1559, however, his family formally adopted Protestantism and he was allowed to return home. He studied law and jurisprudence at Heidelberg in 1565, and Hebrew and German at Padua in 1566.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1559 , Philippe de Mornay became lord of Plessis . He inherited an estate from his maternal great-aunt: Plessis-Marly , in Longvilliers in Yvelines. {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000206601410840&size=large}}
2 CONT [http://patrimoine-de-france.com/yvelines/longvilliers/chateau-1.php ''The Château Plessis-Mornay, formerly Plessis-Marly belonged to Philippe Duplessis-Mornay, diplomat, Calvinist theologian, governor of Saumur.]
2 CONT Mornay joined the army of Conde upon outbreak of the second French War of Religion in 1567, but a fall from his horse prevented him from taking an active part in the campaign. In 1572, he offered his services to the Protestant Ref
2 CONC ormers in France, and collaborated with Gaspard de Coligny in plans for war against Spain. He managed to escape the Massacre of St. Bartholomew's Day of 1572 and took refuge in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to France in 1573, Mornay joined the army of Henry of Navarre and soon became one of Henry's most respected councillors, serving as Henry's diplomatic agent in England (1577-1578 and again in 1580) and in the Low Countri
2 CONC es (1581-1582).[http://resources.huygens.knaw.nl/wvo/app/brief?nr=7465#sthash.hzpgBUFk.dpuf Letter dated 1st of november 1581 written by] https://www.geni.com/people/Willem-van-Oranje/6000000001851827666 representing de "Landraad"
2 CONC , in which he requests  Philippe de Mornay to have captains and officers resorting under colonel 'De la Garde' informed that their wages will be paid properly '''*13''']
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1588, Mornay became leader of the Protestant movement in France, and soon became known as "the Huguenot Pope." He sought to unite all Frenchmen under a religious system that would give free reign to the Reformed Churches or 
2 CONC establish a national church on the Anglican model, and sought to turn royal policy against the Catholic League and against Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the assassination of King Henry III in 1589, Mornay again joined Henry of Navarre's army and participated in all the major campaigns that ultimately led to Henry being crowned King Henry IV. For his service Mornay was ma
2 CONC de Governor of the Huguenot stronghold of Saumur, where he built the greatest of the Huguenot academies. He remained a part of King Henry's circle of advisors until 1593, when the king renounced Protestantism and converted to Cath
2 CONC olicism.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1598, Mornay was a key contributor in the negotiations which led to the Edict of Nantes. Signed by King Henry on April 13, 1598, the Edict gave French Protestants (Huguenots) complete freedom of worship in about 75 towns, as we
2 CONC ll as equal rights with Catholics as citizens.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in 1598, Mornay published De l'institution, usage et doctrine du sainct sacrement de Veucharistie en l'église ancienne, which contained about 5,000 citations from Scripture. In 1600, Jacques Davy Duperron, bishop of Evreux, a
2 CONC ccused him of misquotation in this work. A public disputation was conducted at Fontainebleau, which resulted in his discomfiture, with Henry IV putting finals touches to it.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1601 , worship was established in Plessis by Philippe de Mornay and his wife Charlotte Arbalesque, in accordance with the clause of the Edict of Nantes authorizing two temples per bailiwick. The temple could have been built fro
2 CONC m this date. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mornay opposed Louis XIII's policy of rapprochement with the papacy and with Spain, a position which put him at definite odds with the king. In 1618, he was chosen a deputy to represent the French Protestants at the Synod of Dort,
2 CONC  but was prohibited from attending by Louis XIII. He was removed from his position at Saumur in 1621.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe de Mornay died at his estate of La Foret-sur-Sevre on November 11, 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of His Writings
2 CONT
2 CONT Discours au roi Charles (1572) Remonstrances aux estats pour la paix (1576) Excellent discours de la vie et de la mort (1577) Traité de l'église [Treatise on the Church] (1578) De la vérité de la religion chrestienne (1581) Remons
2 CONC trances à la France (1585) De l'institution, usage et doctrine du sainct sacrement de Veucharistie en l'église ancienne [Concerning the institution, the usage and the doctrine of the Holy Sacrament of the Eucharist in the early Ch
2 CONC urch] (1598) Le Mystère d'iniquité (1611) Mémoires et corresponance (2 vols. 1624-1625)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.robinsonlibrary.com/history/france/france/1515/mornay.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ===See Also===
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_de_Mornay
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/huguenotfamilyin00mornuoft/huguenotfamilyin00mornuoft_djvu.txt
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/protestantexiles01agne#page/n19/mode/2up/search/plessis
2 CONT * https://www.huguenots.fr/2012/10/le-chemin-des-huguenots-le-plessis-mornay-a-la-recherche-dune-communaute-oubliee/
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT =Le Plessis- Marly was the estate of the Duplessis-Mornays,
2 CONT the family which gave the statesman [https://www.geni.com/people/Philippe-de-Mornay/370277724160004066?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1472239666051&through=6000000008714270274 Philippe de Mornay] to the Protestant cause in the troubled 
2 CONC days of Henri IV. Le Plessis-Marly came into Philippe’s possession through his mother [https://www.geni.com/people/Fran%C3%A7oise-Crespin-du-Bec-Dame-de-Buhy-et-du-Grand-Plessis-Marly/6000000008714270274?through=600000001668620204
2 CONC 0 Francoise], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Vice-Admiral-Charles-du-Bec-Crespin-Sieur-de-Bourri-and-de-Wardes/6000000016670658062?through=6000000008714270274 Charles du Bec-Crespin, vice- admiral of France]. Formerly ow
2 CONC ned by her maternal aunt [https://www.geni.com/people/Jeanne-de-Beauvillier/6000000016670566593?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1472239666051&through=6000000008714270274 Jeanne de Deauvilliers], the property was acquired by Francoise in 
2 CONC June 1561.7
2 CONT
2 CONT The church was chosen in 1601 by the royal commissioners Francois d’Angennes and Pierre Jeannin to serve the Calvinists of the Montfort- l’Amaury bailiwick, replacing an earlier place of worship at Garan- cieres-en-Beauce to the s
2 CONC outh-west.8 The Mornays made personal provision in 1606 for the salary of a minister and for the support of the poor. The church was included in the Beauce colloquy of the synodal province for the north-east of France and had clos
2 CONC e connections with the seigneurial church of La Norville in the Hurepoix, sharing the same pastor, Maurice de Lauberon de Montigny, for a number of years after 1626. The Paris temple had been sited in the Hurepoix before 1606, fir
2 CONC st at Grigny and later, in 1599, at Ablon-sur-Seine. both south of the capital, but with the removal to Charenton, Le Plessis-Marly and La Norville alone served the region.9
2 CONT
2 CONT It was for Jansenism, rather than Calvinism, that the Hurepoix was noted in the seventeenth century. The Calvinist reform movement had made little headway there and was very much a minority cult. Jean Jacquart has put forward some
2 CONC  tentative reasons: the ease with which repressive measures could be introduced to counter heresy in towns and villages close to the capital; few complaints of a material kind against the Catholic church and close family ties betw
2 CONC een many of the clergy and their parishioners; social stability in a region which remained relatively strong economically during the wars of religion. Here then was no fertile field for religious innovation and proximity to Paris 
2 CONC strengthened the efforts of Catholic reform: mission priests, following in the footsteps of Vincent de Paul, were active; eucharistic devotions, a counterpoise to Calvinism, were encouraged. A number of landowners returned to the 
2 CONC Catholic faith and those who remained members of the reformed church do not appear to have strongly influenced their tenants.10
2 CONT
2 CONT The anti-Calvinist drive mounted by Louis XIV drove the pastor Jacques Rondeau of Le Plessis-Marly to England,11 while [https://www.geni.com/people/Charles-Marais/6000000001470896580 Charles Marais], his wife [https://www.geni.com
2 CONC /people/Catherine-Tabourdeaux/6000000000429870047?through=6000000001470896580 Catherine Taboureux] and their children [https://www.geni.com/people/Claude-Marais/6000000000430315059?through=6000000001470896580 Claude], [https://www
2 CONC .geni.com/people/Charles-Marais/6000000000573371017?through=6000000001470896580 Charles], [https://www.geni.com/people/Isaac-Marais/6000000007789700488?through=6000000001470896580 Isaac] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Marie-Mara
2 CONC is/6000000007789555922?through=6000000001470896580 Marie-Madeleine] made their way to the United Provinces. Like so many other refugees of the period they had been compelled to accept Catholicism at the revocation, but returned to
2 CONC  the reformed faith in their first country of refuge. Charles, his wife and the older children rejected their forced conversion at the Walloon church in The Hague on September 14, 1687.12
2 CONT
2 CONT (M.Boucher. (1981). French speakers at the Cape:  The  European Background. Pretoria, UNISA p 105-7)
2 CONT
2 CONT === REFERENCES: CHAPTER FIVE
2 CONT * 7. On the Mornay background see HAAG and HAAG, France protestante, VII, pp. 512-542. Le Plessis-Marly is discussed in M. BOUCHER. ‘Cape and company in the early eighteenth century’, Kleio, IX, 1 and 2, June 1977, pp. 67-68.
2 CONT * 8. 5642, Collection Auziere, Ile-de-France, Eglises, L-Z: Le Plessis-Marly, Pays chartrain, p. 23 (Bibl. Prot., SHPF).
2 CONT * 9. JACQUART, Crise rurale, p. 582 and n.; J. PANNIER. ‘Notes sur l’eglise reformee de La Norville; les origines; un registre de 1671; la disparition’, BSHPF, L, April 15, 1901, p. 175.
2 CONT * 10. Crise rurale, pp. 168-169; 583.
2 CONT * 11. MOURS, 'Pasteurs’, BSHPF, CXIV, Jan.-March 1968, p. 81.
2 CONT * 12. AB ZH Gra dtb, ’s-Gravenhage, Lidmaatschap, ens., 1621-1893 (copy): 1225-1227, p. 78, where the names are given as Marets and Taboureur (CBG).
2 CONT *13 [https://www.genealogieonline.nl/genealogie-wiebus/I1060.php letter of Willem van Oranje to Philippe the Mornay to inform officers under De la Garde that they will be paid properly] 
2 CONT
