1 OCCU Conseiller au parlement; Intendant de Provence, du Dauphiné et de Guyenne; Intendant de police, justice et finances; Seigneur de Lauzon, Conseiller au Parlement, maitre des requêtes, intendant, premier directeur des Cent-Associes,
2 CONC  quatrième gouvereur d
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (see French)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Overview=
2 CONT
2 CONT French Governor of New France from 1651 to 1657, one of the most challenging times for the new colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT A prominent lawyer in France, in 1613 Lauzon was appointed a counsellor in the Parlement. He served in several government positions, including president of the Grand Conseil, intendant of [https://www.geni.com/projects/Provence-pr
2 CONC ovince-France/25257 Provence], then of [https://www.geni.com/projects/Guyenne-province-France/25259 Guyenne], and of [https://www.geni.com/projects/Dauphin%C3%A9-province-France/24723 Dauphiné].
2 CONT
2 CONT He had been developing interests in the colony of New France. He was a founding member and became the director of the [https://www.geni.com/projects/Compagnie-des-Cent-Associ%C3%A9s/25382 Co-fondateur de la Compagnie des Cent Asso
2 CONC ciés]. Lauzon used his influence within the company to obtain land for him and his sons in the colony. By 1640, the Lauzons had become the biggest landowners in the colony. Their properties included the Island of Montreal and Île 
2 CONC d'Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lauzon was appointed as [https://www.geni.com/projects/Gouverneurs-de-la-Nouvelle-France-1534-1763/18312 Governor] in 1651. He moved with his three sons—including Francois, the eldest, who was a member of Parlement for Bordeaux --
2 CONC  to the colony. His wife had apparently died previously in France.
